# Next.js build-time artifacts (regenerated automatically)
next-env.d.ts

# Vercel: project link (.vercel/project.json) and the OIDC token it writes to .env.local
.vercel

# local tool state (dev-server preview launch config, not an app deliverable)
.claude/

# Cygwin/Git-Bash crash dumps
*.stackdump

# ── Environment files ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# One block, and it is LAST in this file on purpose. Git resolves ignores by last-matching-pattern,
# so a broad `.env*` written below a `!.env.example` silently defeats the negation — which is exactly
# what happened when `vercel link` appended its own `.env*` to the end of this file. The symptom is
# invisible while the template is already tracked (`git check-ignore` reports nothing for a tracked
# path unless you pass `--no-index`), and only shows up as a missing file in a fresh clone.
#
# What must stay ignored: `.env` and `.env.local`, because that is where SANITY_API_WRITE_TOKEN goes
# — the one credential in this project that can create documents in a live dataset — and where
# `vercel link` writes its OIDC token. This repository is public, and a leak from a public history
# cannot be undone by deleting the commit.
#
# What must stay tracked: `.env.example`, which is placeholders only and is meant to be read.
.env*
!.env.example
